WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:08.747
This video shows how to build the balsa wing of our water-drop aircraft for the Boeing National Aeromodelling Competition.

00:00:08.747 --> 00:00:18.233
The wing has a span of 990 millimetres, a chord of 280 millimetres and an SD7062 airfoil, and it weighs about 157 grams without servos.

00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:28.134
All files, the bill of materials with Indian shop links, and notes on what to ask for in local shops are on the project web page and on GitHub.

00:00:28.134 --> 00:00:31.527
You will find the links at the end of this video.

00:00:32.327 --> 00:00:34.065
Here is the complete wing.

00:00:34.065 --> 00:00:49.571
It is a classic built-up structure: laser-cut ribs every 55 millimetres, a spar with carbon caps, a sheeted leading-edge D-box for torsion stiffness, a false spar that carries the aileron hinges, and 3D-printed PETG servo mounts.

00:00:49.571 --> 00:00:54.383
Each aileron is 325 millimetres long and is driven by an MG90S servo.

00:00:55.183 --> 00:01:12.524
You will need balsa sheets in 1.5, 2, 3 and 6 millimetre thickness, four pultruded carbon strips of 3 by 0.5 millimetres, iron-on covering film, CA hinges, a 1.2 millimetre steel pushrod, PETG filament, and two MG90S servos.

00:01:12.524 --> 00:01:18.980
You also need three glues: thin CA, a PVA wood glue such as Fevicol SH, and epoxy.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:34.400
For tools, you need a laser cutter or a sharp hobby knife, a flat building board at least 1.2 metres long, T-pins, a steel rule, sanding blocks, a covering iron and a heat gun, a scale that reads to 0.1 gram, and a 3D printer.

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:41.325
Before you cut anything, weigh every balsa sheet and divide the mass by its volume to get the density.

00:01:41.325 --> 00:01:51.893
Use the lightest sheets, about 90 kilograms per cubic metre, for the sheeting, medium sheets for the ribs and webs, and the hardest sheets for the spar caps and trailing edges.

00:01:51.893 --> 00:01:54.355
This one step can save ten grams or more.

00:01:55.155 --> 00:01:59.292
Laser cut the ribs and the other flat parts from the DXF files.

00:01:59.292 --> 00:02:04.702
Each file is one sheet of 300 by 100 millimetres, with the grain along the long side.

00:02:04.702 --> 00:02:06.611
Cut only the layer called CUT.

00:02:06.611 --> 00:02:10.939
Make test cuts on scrap first: balsa needs low power and high speed.

00:02:10.939 --> 00:02:16.221
Keep a fire extinguisher near the machine, and never leave the laser running alone.

00:02:17.021 --> 00:02:30.965
Cut the long strips with a steel rule and a sharp knife: two spar caps of 3 by 6 millimetres from hard balsa, the 6 millimetre leading edge, the trailing-edge stock, and 6 millimetre cap strips from 1.5 millimetre sheet.

00:02:30.965 --> 00:02:32.993
The cut list gives every length.

00:02:33.793 --> 00:02:35.296
Three glues do three jobs.

00:02:35.296 --> 00:02:40.671
Thin CA wicks into tight joints and sets in seconds; use it for ribs, cap strips and hinges.

00:02:40.671 --> 00:02:47.376
PVA wood glue gives you time to adjust and sands easily; use it to laminate the tip ribs and to glue the sheeting.

00:02:47.376 --> 00:02:51.423
Epoxy bonds the carbon to the balsa and the printed parts to the wood.

00:02:51.423 --> 00:02:55.006
Mix equal amounts of resin and hardener, and use a thin layer.

00:02:55.006 --> 00:02:58.590
Work in fresh air, and protect your eyes and fingers from CA.

00:02:59.390 --> 00:02:59.973
Step one.

00:02:59.973 --> 00:03:05.677
Glue two carbon strips side by side onto each balsa spar cap with a thin layer of epoxy.

00:03:05.677 --> 00:03:11.511
Put a weight on them, on a flat surface covered with plastic film, and let the epoxy cure.

00:03:12.311 --> 00:03:12.892
Step two.

00:03:12.892 --> 00:03:17.927
Pin the bottom spar cap on the board over the plan, with the carbon face down.

00:03:17.927 --> 00:03:26.706
Support the rib noses on a 3.5 millimetre strip and the rib tails on an 8 millimetre strip, so that every rib sits at the correct angle.

00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:31.418
Slide the ribs onto the spar cap and glue them square, with a set square.

00:03:31.418 --> 00:03:35.808
The ribs at 110 millimetres from the centre are 3 millimetres thick.

00:03:36.608 --> 00:03:37.466
Step three.

00:03:37.466 --> 00:03:48.621
Glue the leading-edge stock to the front of the ribs, then the false spar at 205 millimetres, the aft rib pieces, and the trailing-edge wedges.

00:03:48.621 --> 00:03:52.989
Keep the trailing edge straight with pins on both sides.

00:03:53.789 --> 00:03:54.373
Step four.

00:03:54.373 --> 00:03:58.289
Glue the top spar cap into the rib notches with the carbon face up.

00:03:58.289 --> 00:04:05.886
Then glue the vertical-grain shear webs between the ribs: in front of the spar along the full span, and behind it near the centre.

00:04:05.886 --> 00:04:08.282
The webs make the spar into a strong box.

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:09.683
Step five.

00:04:09.683 --> 00:04:11.005
Sand everything flush.

00:04:11.005 --> 00:04:17.977
Glue on the top D-box sheeting from the leading edge to 80 millimetres, then the centre sheeting and the cap strips.

00:04:17.977 --> 00:04:23.626
Wet the outer face of the sheeting lightly, so that it bends around the nose without cracking.

00:04:24.426 --> 00:04:25.004
Step six.

00:04:25.004 --> 00:04:28.087
Remove the wing from the board and turn it over.

00:04:28.087 --> 00:04:35.151
Add the bottom D-box sheeting, the bottom centre sheeting with the wire exit holes, and the bottom cap strips.

00:04:35.151 --> 00:04:40.224
Then glue the printed servo frames between the ribs at 110 and 165 millimetres.

00:04:41.024 --> 00:04:41.761
Step seven.

00:04:41.761 --> 00:04:49.203
Laminate each tip rib from two 1.5 millimetre layers with the grain crossed, and glue it on at 489 millimetres.

00:04:49.203 --> 00:04:55.437
Add the 3 millimetre ring tip cap, then sand the whole wing smooth with a long sanding block.

00:04:56.237 --> 00:04:56.953
Step eight.

00:04:56.953 --> 00:05:02.943
Build each aileron from its 3 millimetre leading edge, its ribs and the trailing-edge wedge.

00:05:02.943 --> 00:05:08.412
Sand a V bevel on the leading edge so that the aileron can move 20 degrees each way.

00:05:08.412 --> 00:05:14.011
Then cut the four hinge slots in the false spar and in the aileron with a knife blade.

00:05:14.811 --> 00:05:16.972
Cover the wing with iron-on film.

00:05:16.972 --> 00:05:25.811
Iron the edges down first, at about 110 to 120 degrees Celsius or as the film maker says, then shrink the open areas with the heat gun.

00:05:25.811 --> 00:05:31.965
Cover the ailerons separately, and cut the film away over the hinge slots and the servo hatch.

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:33.441
Step nine.

00:05:33.441 --> 00:05:40.882
Push in the CA hinges, check that the aileron moves freely, then wick thin CA into both sides of each hinge.

00:05:40.882 --> 00:05:43.588
Glue the printed horns to the horn ribs.

00:05:43.588 --> 00:05:51.638
Screw each servo to its hatch, connect the 1.2 millimetre pushrod with Z bends, and fix the hatch with four M2 screws.

00:05:52.438 --> 00:05:59.828
Finally, add the rubber-band rub plates at the centre, centre the servos, and set the throws to plus or minus 15 degrees.

00:05:59.828 --> 00:06:01.049
Then weigh the wing.

00:06:01.049 --> 00:06:04.286
The target is less than 160 grams without the servos.

00:06:05.086 --> 00:06:19.822
Our design checks: span 990 millimetres, chord 280 millimetres, spar depth 39.2 millimetres, no overlapping parts, at least 1 millimetre of servo clearance, and a predicted mass of 157 grams.

00:06:20.622 --> 00:06:32.133
Every link for the materials, with notes on what to ask for in local shops and markets, is on the web page and in the GitHub repository: github.com/jagathsrujan/water-drop-rc-wing.

00:06:32.133 --> 00:06:34.608
Thank you for watching, and happy building.
